ARM: OMAP5: enable the C2 retention idle state

The Pyra's kernel tree carries two #if 0 blocks that remove OMAP5's second
cpuidle state, leaving the processor with only C1: wait for an interrupt
with the whole MPU subsystem left powered.  Neither block has a comment and
nothing in the packaging explains them.

Upstream does not do this.  Mainline v5.6 and mainline today both define
the state, added by 7abdb0e23e7b ("ARM: OMAP5: Add basic cpuidle MPU CSWR
support"), whose changelog reports idle power on the omap5-uevm falling
from about 1.7 W to about 920 mW once it is enabled.  The same tree that
disables it here adds a separate driver for DRA7 and leaves that one's
copy of the state switched on, so this was a decision about OMAP5 in
particular rather than a blanket removal.

Take both blocks out, restoring the state to what mainline has.

This is an experiment and it is not part of the Pyra fix set.  A silent
#if 0 around a low-power state is the shape somebody leaves behind when
they find that it hangs, and the failure mode for a broken retention state
is a machine that stops answering with no log.  Build it under its own
release name, boot it one-shot so that it reverts by itself, and do not
carry it into the default kernel until it has survived a long stretch of
ordinary use.

--- a/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
@@ -55,13 +55,11 @@
 		.mpu_state = PWRDM_POWER_ON,
 		.mpu_logic_state = PWRDM_POWER_ON,
 	},
-#if 0
 	{
 		.cpu_state = PWRDM_POWER_RET,
 		.mpu_state = PWRDM_POWER_RET,
 		.mpu_logic_state = PWRDM_POWER_RET,
 	},
-#endif
 };
 
 static struct idle_statedata dra7_idle_data[] = {
@@ -281,7 +279,6 @@
 			.name = "C1",
 			.desc = "CPUx WFI, MPUSS ON"
 		},
-#if 0
 		{
 			/* C2 - CPU0 RET + CPU1 RET + MPU CSWR */
 			.exit_latency = 48 + 60,
@@ -291,7 +288,6 @@
 			.name = "C2",
 			.desc = "CPUx CSWR, MPUSS CSWR",
 		},
-#endif
 	},
 	.state_count = ARRAY_SIZE(omap5_idle_data),
 	.safe_state_index = 0,
